# Tool outputs that the agent itself writes (committed report should be explicit, not transient)
secure-code-report.md
# The JSON report was the one output missing here, so running the CI command
# locally left exactly one untracked file behind — the format most likely to
# be committed by a `git add -A`, since it looks like data rather than a
# generated report.
secure-code-report.json
secure-code-remediation-prompt.md
secure-code-pr-comment.md
secure-code.sarif
secure-code-baseline.tmp.json
# Written by the release workflow's self-audit step.
secure-code-release-audit.md
secure-code-release-audit.json

# Scanner caches
.semgrepignore.local
.bandit.local

# Calibration study working tree. The pinned corpus is cloned here on demand;
# the manifest and the results are the artifacts worth keeping, not the clones.
calibration/.corpus/

# uv's lock file. Nothing in CI or the release reads it — the floor installs
# come from pyproject via `.github/actions/install-floor`, and pyproject is
# already the one source of dependency truth. Committing a lock nothing
# enforces would create a second one, free to drift unnoticed. Delete this
# line the day a workflow actually runs `uv sync`.
uv.lock

# Scan history — trend data, appended every run.
.secure-code/
